      "id": 1,
      "text": "Moonshine earned its name as a simple river landing, a spot marked on old maps rather than a formal town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonshine%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Mid‑18th‑century Acadians fleeing Canada settled Moonshine, making it part of Louisiana’s historic Second Acadian Coast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonshine%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The community sits on the west bank of the Mississippi River, about 40 miles west of New Orleans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonshine%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the antebellum era, Moonshine’s surrounding plantations grew sugarcane, the region’s dominant crop before the Civil War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonshine%2C_Louisiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Moonshine’s culture echoes River Parish traditions, with French Creole roots, Catholic churches, and festivals that celebrate local food and music.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
